Model-authored note overview, sources=['paper.pdf']:
Agentic LLMs survey (arXiv:2503.23037v3), Plaat et al. Definition p.4: agents that receive natural-language input, reason to make decisions and take autonomous actions. Category framework p.4-5: 'The categories reasoning–acting–interacting build upon each other' -> answers categories: reason, act, interact (paper wording reasoning / acting / interacting); category 1 reasoning (reflection, retrieval, decision making), category 2 acting (tools, robots; assistants in medicine/finance), category 3 interacting (multi-agent role-play, open-ended agent societies, emergent behaviour). Literature selection p.5 (Sect. 1.2): 'The papers were initially selected with a Google Scholar search on Agentic LLM. From there, we used a snowballing approach to discover work that was cited but not yet included in our initial set.' -> search_methods: google_scholar_search, snowballing. Only LLM-based work included; non-agentic LLM work used as background; most works 2024, some 2023/2025.
Model-authored note reasoning, sources=['paper.pdf']:
Reasoning = category 1 (pp.7-8): 2.1 Multi-step, 2.2 Self Reflection, 2.3 Retrieval Augmentation. p.10 exact: 'Self Consistency is an ensemble approach that samples diverse reasoning paths, evaluates them, and selects the most consistent answer using majority voting.' -> self_consistency_selection: majority voting. p.13 exact: 'Here, an external control algorithm is created, that calls the model, each time with a different prompt, so that it follows a tree of reasoning steps. When one reasoning path has been traversed, the search backtracks, and tries an alternative. The paper describes both a breadth-first and a depth-first controller.' -> tot_search: breadth_first, depth_first. p.14: 'Self reflection happens when an external algorithm uses the LLM to assess its own predictions, and creates a new prompt for the same LLM to come up with a better answer'; framed as RL where both agent and environment are the LLM with different prompts; needs external memory. p.14-15 PHP, Self Refine (p_gen, p_fb, p_refine), Reflexion (actor/evaluator/reflector), Self Discover, Buffer of Thoughts. p.16 implicit reasoning: Self taught reasoner, DeepSeek-R1/GRPO, RASP program emulation. p.10-11 PoT, PAL, FunSearch, PDDL planners; long chains risk hallucination.
Model-authored note tool_use, sources=['paper.pdf']:
Tool use = category 2 acting (pp.20-25). p.23 exact: 'A range of tools is tested: a calculator, a question-answering system, a search engine, a translation system, and a calendar.' -> toolformer_tools: calculator, question_answering, search_engine, translation_system, calendar. p.23 exact: 'ToolBench [Qin et al., 2023] contains 16,464 APIs from RapidAPI, a large dataset of publicly available REST APIs.' -> toolbench_api_count: 16464. p.24: computer/browser tools (OmniParser V2, Computer Use, Operator, Browser Use), interoperability protocols MCP/ACP/ANP/A2A. p.24-25 safety: AgentHarm 110 malicious tasks; Rainbow Teaming quality-diversity adversarial prompts (MAP-Elites). p.25-26 assistants: AssistantGPT (LLM+tools+planner+memory), meeting assistants, MUCA, thinking assistant.
Model-authored note multi_agent, sources=['paper.pdf']:
Multi-agent interaction = category 3 (Sect. 4, pp.33-47). Role-based interaction: LLM agents exchange natural-language messages (role-play, teamwork, game theory, debate systems such as ChatEval/AutoGen); MAgIC social-deduction games extract Rationality, Judgement, Reasoning, Deception, Self-awareness, Cooperation, Coordination (p.35); Sotopia/GovSim test social goals and cooperation. CAMEL (p.46): user+assistant agents; quote p.46: 'Both agents are given a so-called inception prompt, an initial idea.' -> camel_prompting: inception_prompting. Generative Agents (pp.46-47) add memory, planning, reflection modules; quote p.47: 'Whether the Generative Agents system generates believable behavior is evaluated with the help of 100 human evaluators.' -> generative_agents_evaluators: 100. Simulating 1000 People: 1052 interview agents replicate GSS answers 85% as accurately as humans two weeks later; emergent behaviour, plus erratic/hallucinated behaviour observed.
Model-authored note outlook, sources=['paper.pdf']:
Outlook (Sect. 5, pp. 50-52). Answers: agenda_topics = training_data, hallucination, agent_behavior, self_reflection, safety (Table 4, p.51); shared_foundations = reasoning, self_verification (p.52). P.51 table quote: 'Topic Challenge Training Data Finetune with inference time reasoning data Convergent/stable reinforcement learning VLA, Multimodal signals, such as speech Hallucination Use Self Verification Use Mechanistic Interpretability Use Open Ended/Open World Models for exploration Agent Behavior Scalable simulation infrastructure, role playing Distill reasoning to small models Models of agent and human behavior, emergent behavior, future of society Self reflection In-model self reflection and metareasoning Metacognition, personality Automated Scientific Discovery Safety Assistants: Responsibility, liability Privacy, fairness of data Wider application areas for assistants'. P.52 quote: 'Robotic interaction and tool-use build on grounded retrieval augmentation, social interaction (such as theory of mind) builds on self reflection, and all categories benefit from reasoning and self-verification.' Prose pp.50-51 adds convergent RL training data, hallucination countermeasures, agent-behaviour simulation, in-model reflection and safety/liability.
